---
/**
 * How it works, EN. Twin of `src/pages/processus.astro`.
 *
 * Third person throughout for anything a professional does (rule 1bis): "the
 * contractor excavates down to the footing" is allowed, "excavate down to the
 * footing" is not.
 */
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { pathFor } from '@lib/routes.ts';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '@lib/schema.ts';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const ref = { type: 'static', page: 'process' } as const;
---

<BaseLayout
  locale={locale}
  ref={ref}
  title="How it works — from enquiry to finished work"
  description="The steps between first noticing a foundation problem and the end of the work, and what you should insist on at each one of them along the way."
  schema={[
    breadcrumbSchema(locale, [
      { name: "Home", ref: { type: 'home' } },
      { name: "How it works", ref },
    ]),
  ]}
>
  <section class="contenant bloc-page">
    <div class="mesure">
      <h1>From the first damp patch to the finished work</h1>

      <p class="chapeau">
        Six steps. The most important is the second, and it is the one that almost always gets
        skipped: establishing where the water comes from <em>before</em> authorising any digging.
      </p>

      <h2>1. You document what you can see</h2>
      <p>
        Before calling anyone, note the exact spot, the date, and the conditions: heavy rain,
        snowmelt, or nothing to do with the weather at all. Photograph it, placing a ruler beside
        a crack to keep a sense of scale. These notes cost nothing and change everything: they
        let you compare two quotes against the same observation rather than against two sales
        pitches.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Diagnosis, before the quote</h2>
      <p>
        This is the step that separates a measured expense from a pointless excavation. On a
        drain, a camera inspection says whether it is clogged, crushed or simply loaded with
        sediment — and that distinction separates a cleaning from a full replacement. On a wall,
        it separates a shrinkage crack from structural movement.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>A firm price given over the phone, with nobody having seen the building, should
        make you wary.</strong> The real condition of a wall is only known once it is exposed.
      </p>

      <h2>3. You send your enquiry</h2>
      <p>
        The form passes your description and your area to an independent company holding its own
        RBQ licence. It is free and commits you to nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>4. The contractor visits and prices the work</h2>
      <p>
        They examine the structure, establish what is happening, and issue a written quote. This
        is the moment to ask for the licence number and check it yourself in the public register
        of the Régie du bâtiment du Québec — a foundation job often touches three separate
        subcategories, and being licensed to dig does not authorise waterproofing.
      </p>

      <h2>5. You compare, then you decide</h2>
      <p>
        A comparable quote names the standard used and which part of it, the type of pipe, what
        site reinstatement includes, and what would trigger an extra charge. Two bare totals
        cannot be compared. You remain free to seek other quotes, and doing so is recommended.
        The <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'pricing' }, locale)}>pricing page</a> sets
        out the five points to insist on.
      </p>

      <h2>6. The work, and what is left afterwards</h2>
      <p>
        The contractor carries out the work, reinstates the ground, and gives you their
        warranties in writing. Keep the inspection report, the quote and the photographs: on a
        building under five years old, that file is what makes a claim possible at all. The
        <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'faq' }, locale)}>frequently asked questions</a>
        explain the warranty windows.
      </p>

      <h2>What this service does not do</h2>
      <p>
        Solage Capitale performs no work and supervises no site. The contract is formed between
        you and the company that visits. Nothing here constitutes technical advice or an
        engineering opinion — see the
        <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'terms' }, locale)}>terms of use</a>.
      </p>

      <p class="cta-fin">
        <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'quote' }, locale)}>Request a quote</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
